Root cause:
When a species is assigned a name derived from a primary thermo library (via `spe.setName(spe.getThermoData().getName())`), the code updated the `Species` object's name field but failed to regenerate the Chemkin-formatted NASA polynomial thermo data. This NASA thermo data is stored as a pre-formatted string/block that embeds the species name directly within it. Since this cached representation was created earlier (before the name change) using the old, default, or auto-generated species name, it became stale and out of sync with the newly updated `name` field. As a result, when the Chemkin file was written, the thermo section still displayed the old species name (from the outdated cached NASA polynomial data), while other parts of the output correctly used the new name — causing a mismatch/inconsistency in the generated Chemkin file.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`spe = new Species(id,name,p_chemGraph);`
`spe.setName(spe.getThermoData().getName());`
Reason: When the `Species` object is constructed, its Chemkin-formatted NASA polynomial thermo data is generated and cached using the initial (default/auto-generated) name. Later, when the species is renamed via `setName()` to the name derived from the primary thermo library, only the `name` field is updated — the cached NASA polynomial string, which embeds the species name, is not regenerated. This leaves the NASA thermo data stale, still containing the old species name.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`spe.generateNASAThermoData();`
Reason: This statement forces regeneration of the Chemkin-formatted NASA polynomial thermo data immediately after the species name is changed, ensuring the cached thermo block reflects the new name. This keeps the NASA polynomial data consistent with the updated `name` field so that the Chemkin output file correctly shows the new species name in both the thermo section and elsewhere.


